@title AWS landing zone
@subtitle Cloud Foundations, Meridian Logistics
@date 2026-10-05

## Account structure

diagram lz dir=LR title="Landing zone accounts"

lz: orgs "AWS Organizations" svc
lz: sso "IAM Identity Center" svc
lz: guard "GuardDuty" svc
lz: hub "Security Hub" svc
lz: logs "Log Archive Bucket" store
lz: tgw "Transit Gateway" net
lz: nat "NAT Gateway" net
lz: prodapp "Prod App Subnets" net
lz: proddata "Prod Data Subnets" net
lz: devapp "Dev App Subnets" net
lz: group egress "Egress VPC" nat
lz: group prodvpc "Prod VPC" prodapp, proddata
lz: group devvpc "Dev VPC" devapp
lz: group mgmt "Management account" orgs, sso
lz: group sec "Security account" guard, hub
lz: group logarch "Log Archive account" logs
lz: group netacct "Network account" tgw, egress
lz: group prodacct "Prod workload account" prodvpc
lz: group devacct "Dev workload account" devvpc
lz: prodvpc -> tgw : attachment
lz: devvpc -> tgw : attachment
lz: tgw -> nat : 0.0.0.0/0
lz: guard -> hub : findings
lz: orgs -> logs : CloudTrail logs

## Network ranges

table = VPC,Account,CIDR,Region; Egress VPC,Network,10.0.0.0/20,us-west-2; Prod VPC,Prod workload,10.10.0.0/16,us-west-2; Dev VPC,Dev workload,10.20.0.0/16,us-west-2

## Guardrails

Service control policies in force:

- Deny leaving the organization.
- Deny disabling CloudTrail.
- Deny regions other than us-west-2 and us-east-1.
- Deny root user access keys.

! Prod and Dev VPCs cannot route to each other. The Transit Gateway uses separate route tables, rt-prod and rt-dev.
